---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Spidergraph — Broker Removal Tracker"
  description="Track your own opt-out requests across major people-search sites — direct links to each site's official removal page, tracked locally in your browser."
  path="broker-removal-tracker/"
  current="broker-tracker"
  accent="red"
>
  <link slot="head" rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}shared/tool-page.css`} />
  <link
    slot="head"
    rel="stylesheet"
    href={`${base}pages/broker-removal-tracker.css`}
  />

  <div class="wrap">
    <header>
      <div>
        <div class="eyebrow">Self-audit · 16 broker requests tracked</div>
        <h1>
          Broker <span>Removal Tracker</span>
        </h1>
        <div class="sub">
          Search your own name on each site below, then use that site's own
          official opt-out page to request removal. Check it off once submitted
          — nothing here is sent anywhere, this is just a local checklist for
          your own follow-through.
        </div>
      </div>
      <button type="button" class="reset-btn" id="resetBtn">
        Reset tracker
      </button>
    </header>

    <div class="intro-card">
      <b>Why this exists:</b> people-search sites like these compile profiles —
      name, address history, relatives, phone numbers, sometimes court records —
      from public records and data-broker feeds, then sell access to anyone
      searching a name. Opting out at the source removes a listing until the
      site's next data refresh; it doesn't stop the underlying record from
      existing. See <b>Domain 8: OSINT & Digital Footprint</b> in the Playbook
      for the full explanation of how these sites source data and why removals
      don't stick permanently.
      <div class="links">
        <a href="../playbook/#8-osint-digital-footprint">
          📖 Read the full explainer in the Playbook →
        </a>
        <a href="../privacy-stack-builder/#domain-digital-footprint">
          ⚙️ Compare automated removal services (EasyOptOuts / DeleteMe /
          Optery) →
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="layout">
      <div>
        <div class="list-head">
          <span class="list-head-title">Sites</span>
        </div>
        <div id="brokerList"></div>
      </div>

      <div class="sidebar">
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-title">Progress</div>
          <div class="score-row">
            <div>
              <div class="score-num" id="scoreNum">
                0%
              </div>
              <div class="score-label">requests submitted</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-title">Summary</div>
          <div class="metric">
            <span class="k">Requested</span>
            <span class="v teal" id="mDone">
              0/16
            </span>
          </div>
          <div class="metric">
            <span class="k">Not started</span>
            <span class="v red" id="mTodo">
              16
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-title">Long tail</div>
          <p class="small">
            This list covers the ~16 highest-traffic broker sites. Dozens more
            exist. For the full, actively-maintained free list, use Bazzell's
            workbook or EFF's opt-out guide.
          </p>
          <a
            class="card-link"
            href="https://inteltechniques.com/workbook.html"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener"
          >
            IntelTechniques workbook ↗
          </a>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-title">Companion tool</div>
          <p class="small">
            This tracks people-search listings of your own data. If it's a
            breach of an account instead of a broker profile, that's the Breach
            Check Tracker's job, not this one.
          </p>
          <a class="card-link" href="../breach-check-tracker/">
            Breach Check Tracker →
          </a>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-title">Export</div>
          <button type="button" id="exportBtn" class="reset-btn">
            Export progress ↓
          </button>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-title">History</div>
          <p class="small">
            Save a dated checkpoint to compare your progress over time.
          </p>
          <button
            type="button"
            id="snapshotBtn"
            class="reset-btn"
            style="width:100%; margin-bottom:12px;"
          >
            Save snapshot
          </button>
          <div id="historyList" class="history-list"></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <footer>
      <b>Companion pages:</b> the "why" behind this list lives in Domain 8 of
      the
      <b>Playbook</b>; if you'd rather pay a service to do this on a recurring
      cycle instead of manually, compare EasyOptOuts / DeleteMe / Optery in the{' '}
      <b>Stack Builder</b> (slot OSN-01). Opt-out URLs and processes change —
      each entry below shows the date it was last checked; report a broken link
      by opening an issue on the repo. Your checked/unchecked state stays in
      this browser only, in <code>localStorage</code> — nothing is sent
      anywhere, and no name, address, or search input is ever collected by this
      page.
    </footer>
  </div>

  <script src={`${base}shared/persist.js`} is:inline></script>
  <script src={`${base}shared/history.js`} is:inline></script>
  <script src={`${base}pages/broker-removal-tracker.js`} is:inline></script>
</BaseLayout>
